a really common question we always hear  is like how the hell do you go about  managing your time and setting like  almost boundaries right because you want  you want to make your team feel like  you're there to help and you're there to  support them but at the same time you  don't want to be a manager who like  always like gives them the answers  rather than them being given the space  to like find the answers themselves  how how can people go about yeah just  just creating those boundaries and  better managing their time so they can  perform but also give that space to  their team yeah i think it needs to be a  safe place that people come back to you  and go hey i'm struggling or you know  i need help but i think  things like day plans evaluating the day  before being really militantly a day  plan saying what are you trying to  achieve yeah breaking sometimes breaking  up with sprints and saying hey look i  want to achieve this the next couple of  hours cool off you go if you need me i'm  over here yeah um  let's let's review how you got on and  and doing it that way so  i think um  sitting next to someone and watching  them all day is not the answer i think  just being really clear around what that  person is looking to achieve giving them  some guidance around it and then  follow-up is the most underrated thing  in recruitment follow-up i mean if  you're not going to follow up you might  as well not bother setting the person up  in the first place so what so  if me and you sit down in the morning  and you're my manager and say right then  the this is my day plan this is what i'm  aiming to achieve by the end of the day  you're saying like are you making sure  that you tie that together by going  right how do you do that i mean there'll  be  a hundred thousand recruiters that get  set up every day because it's the  manager's job but how often do they sit  down and go through like how did it go  what did you learn what do you need from  me  asking those key questions  [Music]  you
 

